# Extending Laravel's Auth Facade: Adding a Custom Role Check

In Laravel 5, the `Auth` facade is a static proxy that delegates to an underlying authentication guard. By default, it exposes methods like `check()`, `user()`, and `id()`. However, you might need to add domain-specific logic directly to this facade without modifying the core framework or creating global helper functions that clutter your namespace. A common requirement is implementing a `hasRole` check within middleware, but attempting to extend the `Auth` facade itself requires understanding how Laravel's service container and facades interact.

The source material presents a scenario where the developer wants to define `Auth::hasRole()`. The immediate technical hurdle is that the `Auth` facade does not allow for arbitrary method additions at runtime in the way a class trait or interface might. The facade is essentially a static wrapper around the `Illuminate\Auth\Factory` instance stored in the application container. To add a new method like `hasRole`, you must register a custom resolver for the `auth` service that returns an object implementing your extended interface, or more commonly, define the logic within your own middleware as shown in the provided example.

Looking at the provided middleware implementation:

```php
namespace App\Http\Middleware\Auth;

use Closure;
use Illuminate\Contracts\Auth\Guard;

class AdminAuthenticate
{
    protected $auth;

    public function __construct(Guard $auth)
    {
        $this->auth = $auth;
    }

    public function handle($request, Closure $next, $role)
    {
        if ($this->auth->guest()) {
            if ($request->ajax()) {
                return response('Unauthorized.', 401);
            } else {
                return redirect()->guest('login');
            }
        }

        if (auth()->check() && auth()->user()->hasRole($role)) {
            return $next($request);
        }

        // Missing explicit deny logic in the original snippet
    }
}
```

The code correctly injects the `Guard` interface via the constructor. This allows access to `$this->auth`, which is typically an instance of `Illuminate\Auth\GuardHelpers`. The critical line is `auth()->user()->hasRole($role)`. This assumes that your `User` model has a trait or method named `hasRole`. In standard Laravel 5 setups, the `Auth` facade itself cannot simply be extended to include `hasRole`. Instead, you achieve this pattern by extending the User model.

If you attempt to add `Auth::hasRole()` globally via a trait on the `Illuminate\Auth\Facade`, it is not supported out of the box. The facade relies on specific signatures defined in `Illuminate\Support\Facades\Auth`. To get a method that feels like `Auth::hasRole()`, you would need to create a custom alias or helper function, but this breaks the principle of keeping core logic isolated.

A more robust approach, which aligns with Laravel's architecture, is to ensure your `User` model accepts the role check. You can add a trait to your user model:

```php
namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Auth\Authenticatable;
use Illuminate\Contracts\Auth\Authenticatable as AuthenticatableContract;
use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

class User extends Model implements AuthenticatableContract {
    use Authenticatable, HasRoleTrait; // Assuming a trait exists
}
```

Then, inside your middleware, you rely on the user object:

```php
if (auth()->check() && auth()->user()->hasRole($role)) {
    return $next($request);
}
```

The original snippet is missing an explicit `else` block to handle unauthorized users. In Laravel middleware, if you do not call `$next($request)` and do not return a response, the request may continue or throw a warning depending on the context. If the user is authenticated but lacks the role, you should redirect or return a 403:

```php
else {
    if ($request->ajax()) {
        return response('Unauthorized.', 403);
    } else {
        return redirect()->guest('login');
    }
}
```

Finally, regarding the route definition `Route::group(['namespace' => 'Admin', 'middleware' => 'timesheets.admin:Administrator'], ...)`, ensure that the middleware class is actually registered in your `app/Http/Kernel.php` under the `route` array with the name `timesheets.admin`. If this registration is missing, the route group will fail to bind the middleware, resulting in an error when the request hits the `home` route.

For complex role-based logic, consider using packages like Laravel RoleManager or implementing a dedicated policy system if you are on a version that supports it. However, for vanilla Laravel 5, extending the User model and checking properties within your custom middleware remains the standard, maintainable pattern.
